//
// Expected: a1 — and the trace must carry the shared-freshness note.
//
// This model pins the direction of the session model that is easy to forget.
// Sessions of a role here share that role's generated constants, and the usual
// consequence is a missed attack. The other consequence is this one: the engine
// admits a trace that unbounded replication would not.
//
// Bob keys w's envelope on a nonce he generates, so a ciphertext under
// HASH(k, n) is the only thing his AEAD_DEC will open. He later emits v under
// the same key. In this engine, session 2 of Bob decrypts v happily, because
// session 2's n is session 1's n; w is then attacker-authored and successfully
// used, so the authentication query fails. Under `!P` session 2's key is
// HASH(k, n_2), v was made under HASH(k, n_1), the decryption fails and no
// attacker-authored value is ever used: there is no attack.
//
// The verdict stands as a statement about this engine's semantics. What must
// not regress is the note, which is what tells the reader that the witness rests
// on an equality replication would not grant.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public p
]

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	generates n
]

Bob -> Alice: n

principal Alice[
	ka = HASH(k, n)
	w = AEAD_ENC(ka, p, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: w

principal Bob[
	kb = HASH(k, n)
	pb = AEAD_DEC(kb, w, nil)?
]

principal Alice[
	generates u
]

Alice -> Bob: u

principal Bob[
	v = AEAD_ENC(kb, u, nil)
]

Bob -> Alice: v

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: w
]
